            <ArticleTitle>Slow injection of prostaglandin E1 decreases associated penile pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>903-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">A randomized, double-blind study to determine whether speed of intracavernous injection of prostaglandin E1 (PGE1) is associated with pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">On two separate occasions, using two different speeds of injection (5 versus 60 seconds), 11 subjects received the same dose of PGE1 that they used at home. The presence, duration, and intensity of pain were recorded.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">We found that fast penile injection of PGE1 was associated with a greater intensity of pain (P = 0.04).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Slow injection of PGE1 is less often associated with penile pain, the most common adverse effect of this treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
